/*! @brief multibase is an encoding method that introduces multiple encoding prefixes based on Base1x encoding

multibase can encode the same data using different encoding methods and add a prefix to indicate the encoding method. multibase supports a total of 15 encoding methods, namely base1, base2, base8, base10, base16, base32, base32hex, base32z, base36, base40, base56, base58flickr, base58btc, base64, base64url. Among them, base16, base32 and base64 are more commonly used.

multibase can be used to change the display form of binary data without changing the binary data itself. For example, encode a randomly generated binary data into a base32 format string:
```JavaScript
const {
    encode
} = require('multibase');

const crypto = require('crypto');
const data = crypto.randomBytes(10); // generate 10 bytes random data
const encodedStr = encode(data, 'base32'); // encode data to base32 string
console.log(encodedStr); // ==> "bpgwnvztqmlbo5fy"
```
Decode the above string to the original binary data:
```JavaScript
const {
    decode
} = require('multibase');

const data = decode('bpgwnvztqmlbo5fy', 'base32'); // decode base32 string to data
console.log(data); // ==> <Buffer a7 55 3d 33 ca 97 ac 0d aa 40>
```
As you can see, through multibase, we encode the original binary data into a base32 format string, and can decode this string back to the original binary data.
 */
module multibase
{
    /*! @brief Encode data in multibase format
	 @param data Data to encode
	 @param codec Specify encoding method
	 @return Return the encoded string
	 */
    static String encode(Buffer data, String codec);

    /*! @brief Decode string to binary data in multibase format
	 @param data String to decode
	 @return Return the decoded binary data
	 */
    static Buffer decode(String data);
};